<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.3: The Coordinates of the Vertex</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02012</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.3: The Coordinates of the Vertex</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b8f56081-84a1-4105-9731-f3bf254645c8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(y=x^2\). Then, add different numbers to \(x\) before it is squared (for example, \(y=(x+4)^2\), \(y=(x−3)^2\)) and observe how the graph changes.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Adding a number to \(x\) moves the graph to the left. Subtracting a number (or adding a negative number) moves the graph to the right. The vertex stays on the \(x\)-axis.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(y=(x−1)^2\). Then, experiment with each of the following changes to the function and see how they affect the graph and the vertex.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Add different constant terms to \((x−1)^2\) (for example: \(y=(x−1)^2+5\), \(y=(x−1)^2−9)\). </item>
</list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem></exercise>
</example><div class="os-raise-ib-content" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="Reveal2">
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Adding a constant term moves it up and subtracting moves it down. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex doesn't change.</para>
</div>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Multiplying \((x−1)^2\) by different coefficients (for example: \(y=3(x−1)^2\), \(y=-2(x−1)^2\), \(y=0.5(x−1)^2\). </para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para> Multiplying the squared term by a number doesn't change the vertex, but it changes the opening of the graph to "wider" or "narrower," or changes the direction of the graph to open upward or downward.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="f0b34626-53c2-444d-bdc8-ff755e0788a6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>Without graphing, find the coordinates of the vertex of \(y=(x+10)^2\) and determine if it opens up or down.</para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is -10.</para><para>The correct answer is is -10. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is -10.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="78115021-dd69-4349-a6b0-ee2dbb7a4169" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 0.</para><para>The correct answer is is 0. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 0.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="fa086711-1ff1-437c-a416-95dafb171f2b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Does the graph open up or down? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para><para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Up. The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<example><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to see the solutions to question 3 organized into a table.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>
          Equation
        </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
          Coordinates of vertex
        </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
          Graph opens up or down?
        </emphasis></entry></row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
          \(y=(x+10)^2\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \((-10, 0)\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
          Up
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="888b0421-a3b8-4c69-954a-7b61207524da" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>Without graphing, find the coordinates of the vertex of \(y=(x−4)^2+8\) and determine if it opens up or down.</para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 4.</para><para>The correct answer is is 4. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 4.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="bbb6f97f-b144-4be0-89e9-52604b454621" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex? </para>
      </problem><solution><para> Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 8.</para><para>The correct answer is is 8. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 8.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="798d9134-c715-4e30-8eda-95ddc060c136" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Does the graph open up or down? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para><para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Up. The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<example><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to see the solutions to questions 3 – 4 organized into a table.</para>
  </problem><solution>
