</example><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Are the patterns you noticed still happening? See if you can apply those patterns to the following equations.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="8fd2bb2b-35a9-49b3-93e0-6b1010b68b27" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–3\).</para><para>The correct answer is is \(–3\). The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–3\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="97021ac7-5208-432a-a6f5-432c6448485f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(-5\).</para><para>The correct answer is is \(n\). The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(-5\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="1d9cfcdf-595c-471d-9307-16dd20ce2f36" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), does the graph open up or down?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient \(\frac{1}{2})\). </para><para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Up.The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient \(\frac{1}{2})\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="16199d79-2505-408f-b4ff-e5b931fa83bb" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–100\).</para><para>The correct answer is is \(–100\). The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–100\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="a1771807-d7bb-465d-9019-eabb1fce4737" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(50\).</para><para>The correct answer is is \(n\). The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(50\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="7fc8f227-f6be-48a3-97bf-c9787f891f76" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), does the graph open up or down? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph opens downward. The squared term has a negative coefficient \((-1)\).. </para><para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Up. The graph opens downward. The squared term has a negative coefficient \((-1)\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
